   SSiiggnnaall HHaannddlliinngg RReeqquuiirreemmeennttss
     Applications using PMCs are required to handle the following signals:

     SIGBUS  When the hwpmc(4) module is unloaded using kldunload(8), pro‐
             cesses that have PMCs allocated to them will be sent a SIGBUS
             signal.

     SIGIO   The hwpmc(4) driver will send a PMC owning process a SIGIO signal
             if:

             ••   any process-mode PMC allocated by it loses all its target
                 processes.

             ••   the driver encounters an error when writing log data to a
                 configured log file.  This error may be retrieved by a subse‐
                 quent call to ppmmcc__fflluusshh__llooggffiillee().

   TTyyppiiccaall PPrrooggrraamm FFllooww
     1.   An application would first invoke function ppmmcc__iinniitt() to allow the
          library to initialize itself.

     2.   Signal handling would then be set up.

     3.   Next the application would allocate the PMCs it desires using func‐
          tion ppmmcc__aallllooccaattee().

     4.   Initial values for PMCs may be set using function ppmmcc__sseett().

     5.   If a log file is necessary for the PMCs to work, it would be config‐
          ured using function ppmmcc__ccoonnffiigguurree__llooggffiillee().

     6.   Process scope PMCs would then be attached to their target processes
          using function ppmmcc__aattttaacchh().

     7.   The PMCs would then be started using function ppmmcc__ssttaarrtt().

     8.   Once started, the values of counting PMCs may be read using function
          ppmmcc__rreeaadd().  For PMCs that write events to the log file, this logged
          data would be read and parsed using the pmclog(3) family of func‐
          tions.

     9.   PMCs are stopped using function ppmmcc__ssttoopp(), and process scope PMCs
          are detached from their targets using function ppmmcc__ddeettaacchh().

     10.  Before the process exits, it may release its PMCs using function
          ppmmcc__rreelleeaassee().  Any configured log file may be closed using function
          ppmmcc__ccoonnffiigguurree__llooggffiillee().

EEVVEENNTT SSPPEECCIIFFIIEERRSS
     Event specifiers are strings comprising of an event name, followed by op‐
     tional parameters modifying the semantics of the hardware event being
     probed.  Event names are PMC architecture dependent, but the PMC library
     defines machine independent aliases for commonly used events.

     Event specifiers spellings are case-insensitive and space characters, pe‐
     riods, underscores and hyphens are considered equivalent to each other.
     Thus the event specifiers "Example Event", "example-event", and
     "EXAMPLE_EVENT" are equivalent.

